        "problem": "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ if $y=\\frac{3sec\u2061x}{{e}^{x}}.$",
        "worked_solution": "We calculate the derivative using the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3sec\u2061x}{{e}^{x}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(3sec\u2061xtan\u2061x)({e}^{x})-(3sec\u2061x)({e}^{x})}{({e}^{x})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{x}sec\u2061x(tan\u2061x-1)}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3sec\u2061x(tan\u2061x-1)}{{e}^{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
